why 12 1/2 CADENCE The melody is in the Phrygian mode and ends on E, so Bach ends on the E major chord, which in A minor is V.
why 1 0 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to A while the tenor keeps its A from the upbeat. The doubled third keeps the held note.
why 1 1/2 CROSSING The alto drops to A under the tenor's D and climbs back A–B–C. The melody is low here, so the inner voices have little room and cross for a beat.
why 1 5/8 CROSSING The alto climbs back past B; on the next beat it is above the tenor again.
why 2 1/2 LEAPS The bass rises F♯–G and leaps a fourth to C at the fermata. The tritone F♯–C spans two chords, so it is not heard as one interval.
why 4 7/8 CROSSING The tenor passes down A–G–F♯ under the bass's held A for an eighth; on the next beat the bass drops to D♯.
why 9 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and alto (A–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides; the alto moves on to G at once.
